# Milestone 2: Multi-Source Requirement Ingestion and Multimodal Document Extraction

## Project Context

I am building an Autonomous Software Testing Agent called Autonomous QA Studio.

The existing application has:
- A frontend dashboard.
- A Python FastAPI backend.
- OpenAI integration for generating test cases from plain-text requirements.
- An existing text-based test generation API.

I now want to extend the backend so users can upload multiple types of software requirement documents, including documents containing both text and images.

The objective of this milestone is to build a reliable document ingestion and extraction engine that prepares content for future AI-based requirement understanding and test case generation.

## 1. First Inspect the Existing Project

Before making any changes:

1. Inspect the complete repository structure.
2. Understand the existing FastAPI backend architecture.
3. Inspect existing API endpoints, request schemas and response schemas.
4. Understand how the frontend communicates with the backend.
5. Identify existing file handling and storage mechanisms.
6. Identify existing dependencies and reusable services.
7. Preserve existing functionality.

Important:
- Do not replace the existing text-based test generation implementation.
- Do not break existing APIs.
- Do not create duplicate backend projects.
- Reuse existing components wherever possible.
- Follow the existing project structure and coding conventions.

## 2. Supported File Formats

The system must support the following input formats:

| File Type | Extensions |
|---|---|
| PDF | .pdf |
| Word | .docx |
| Excel | .xlsx |
| PowerPoint | .pptx |
| CSV | .csv |
| Text | .txt |
| Images | .png, .jpg, .jpeg |

Users must be able to upload multiple files in a single request.

A document must not be treated as text-only simply because its extension is PDF, DOCX or PPTX. It may contain screenshots, diagrams, wireframes and other visual information.

## 3. File Upload API

Create an endpoint:

POST /api/requirements/upload

The endpoint should:

- Accept multiple files using multipart/form-data.
- Accept an optional project ID if project support already exists.
- Validate extensions and MIME types.
- Validate actual file signatures wherever practical.
- Enforce configurable file size and file count limits.
- Reject unsupported, corrupted and empty files.
- Generate unique server-side file identifiers.
- Process files independently.
- Return processing status for each file.
- Handle partial failures without losing successful results from other files.

Do not allow arbitrary filenames or file paths to be used for filesystem access.

## 4. Document Content Extraction

Implement separate, reusable extraction services for each format.

### A. PDF Processing

Use PyMuPDF or another appropriate PDF processing library.

Requirements:

- Extract text page by page.
- Preserve page numbers.
- Extract embedded images wherever possible.
- Preserve image-to-page relationships.
- Detect pages with little or no extractable text.
- Identify scanned pages.
- Support rendering complete pages as images when necessary.
- Preserve tables and reading order as accurately as practical.
- Avoid unnecessary duplicate image extraction.

For scanned pages:
- Mark them as requiring OCR.
- Prepare page images for future OCR and vision-model processing.
- Do not silently report empty scanned pages as successfully extracted.

### B. DOCX Processing

Use python-docx and appropriate DOCX package relationship handling.

Requirements:

- Extract headings.
- Extract paragraphs.
- Extract tables.
- Extract embedded images.
- Preserve document order as accurately as possible.
- Preserve image placement and relationships.
- Extract image captions where possible.
- Associate images with nearby headings and descriptive paragraphs.
- Preserve section information.

Important:
DOCX images are stored as relationships in the underlying document package. Ensure that embedded images are actually extracted rather than only extracting text through python-docx.

### C. XLSX Processing

Use openpyxl.

Requirements:

- Extract workbook sheet names.
- Extract non-empty rows and cell values.
- Preserve row and column information.
- Extract tables and validation rules where practical.
- Extract embedded images when present.
- Preserve the sheet name and image location.

### D. CSV Processing

Requirements:

- Extract headers and rows.
- Handle different encodings.
- Handle malformed files safely.
- Preserve row numbers and column names.

### E. TXT Processing

Requirements:

- Extract plain text.
- Preserve meaningful line breaks.
- Handle encoding issues safely.

### F. PPTX Processing

Use python-pptx.

Requirements:

- Extract slide titles and text.
- Extract tables.
- Extract embedded images.
- Preserve slide numbers.
- Preserve image positions.
- Associate images with relevant slide text.

### G. Standalone Image Processing

Supported:
- PNG
- JPG
- JPEG

Requirements:

- Validate image format and dimensions.
- Check file size.
- Generate unique image identifiers.
- Preserve original image files.
- Prepare images for future multimodal AI processing.
- Do not fabricate image descriptions.
- Do not claim vision analysis has been completed.

## 5. Embedded Image and Screenshot Extraction

This is a critical requirement.

Documents can contain:
- Application screenshots.
- UI mockups.
- Wireframes.
- Flowcharts.
- Architecture diagrams.
- Business process diagrams.
- Other visual references.

The system must extract these images even when they are embedded inside PDF, DOCX, PPTX or XLSX files.

For every extracted image, maintain metadata similar to:

{
  "image_id": "IMG_001",
  "filename": "registration_screen.png",
  "source_document": "Registration_SRS.docx",
  "source_location": {
    "section": "Registration Requirements",
    "page": null,
    "slide": null,
    "sheet": null
  },
  "position": {
    "order": 4
  },
  "associated_text": null,
  "processing_status": "pending_vision_analysis"
}

Adapt the schema to the actual implementation.

Additional requirements:

- Preserve original document references.
- Preserve image order and position where possible.
- Extract captions and nearby descriptive text.
- Associate images with relevant sections where possible.
- Do not assume every nearby paragraph describes the image.
- Mark uncertain associations for future review.
- Keep original extracted images available for future AI processing.

If a PDF contains a full-page screenshot rather than a separately embedded image, support page rendering so the visual content is not lost.

## 6. Context Association Engine

Build a reusable mechanism that connects extracted content.

For example:

Document: Registration_SRS.docx

Section: User Registration

Text:
"Users should be able to register using their email and password."

Image:
IMG_001

Caption:
"Registration screen"

The output should preserve the relationship between the section, text and image.

The association mechanism should consider:

- Document reading order.
- Section headings.
- Nearby paragraphs.
- Image captions.
- Tables.
- Page numbers.
- Slide numbers.

Do not make unsupported assumptions about image meaning.

Keep uncertain associations explicitly marked for review.

